## The blocking defect (Red test, immutable)

Test: __tests__/storytime-loops-checks.test.ts, "loops-recall > checks a sound and a tricky card against the taught set", line 483 to 487.

    const trickyIn = loopComplete(1);
    const trickyOk = recallItems(trickyIn, 2);
    trickyOk[1] = { kind: 'tricky', text: 'can', week: 1 };
    ... expectPass(reportFor(trickyIn), 'loops-recall', 'the taught tricky word can');

The w01 recall list is [sound n, tricky a, tricky and, tricky can, frame]. The test sets index 1 to `can`.
Day 2 then holds `can` at index 1 and at index 3. Decision Q6 says that two items repeat when kind and stem(text) are equal in one day.
FR-6 says "No recall item repeats inside one day". The same test file asserts this rule in "reports a repeated item in one day".
So the check must report the repeat and the test cannot pass. No honest rule separates the two cases.

Proof: jr-green-0b368463-probe.ts and jr-green-0b368463-probe-out.txt:

    items of day 2: ["sound:n","tricky:can","tricky:and","tricky:can","frame:Hello, I am ___."]
    loops-recall: warn day 2: "can" (tricky) repeats
    variant with can once: pass

Requested Mid Red retry: change the test so that day 2 holds `can` once. For example, keep `trickyOk[1] = can` and set
`trickyOk[3] = { kind: 'tricky', text: 'a', week: 1 }`. With that data the check passes (probe variant).
The other 31 tests of the file and the 27 schema tests pass with my code.

